}] "Shooting 3: Prone at 9.0 km, 50 m range" [:br {}] "Shooting 4: Standing at 12.0 km, 50 m range" [:br {}] "Total Climbing: 490 m"]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "Difficult windy conditions greeted the women biathletes for the 15 km individual event, which affected the shooting. This cost one favorite, German " [:a {:href "/athletes/80953"} "Uschi Disl"] ", who missed five shots and would finish 12th. The defending champion was " [:a {:href "/athletes/101160"} "Andrea Henkel"] " who had won the event at the 2005 World Championships. She was on course to defend her gold medal until she missed her final two shots, her only misses, and dropped to fourth. The final standings initially were taken by two Russians, with " [:a {:href "/athletes/101164"} "Svetlana Ishmuratova"] " first and " [:a {:href ""} "Olga Pyleva"] " second. But Pyleva would be disqualified for a doping offense when her urine test showed traces of carphedon, a stimulant that could increase endurance and tolerance to cold. Pyleva admitted to taking an over-the-counter medication to treat an ankle injury, and it was found that it contained the carphedon, although it was not listed in the ingredients of the medication. After her disqualification, German " [:a {:href "/athletes/101163"} "Martina Glagow"] " moved up to silver, while Russian " [:a {:href "/athletes/99555"} "Albina Akhatova"] " won the bronze. Ishmuratova’s gold was an upset. She had been on the Russian team since 1996, winning three gold medals in relays at the World Championships, but individually had only a pursuiit bronze and mass start silver at the 2003 Worlds. She had not yet placed in the top 10 in the seasonal World Cup standings."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Adjusted Time"] [:th {} "Misses"] [:th {} "Skiing"] [:th {} "Shooting 1 Misses"] [:th {} "Shooting 1"] [:th {} "Shooting 2 Misses"] [:th {} "Shooting 2"] [:th {} "Shootin